    takeaway: `The central insight of information geometry is that parameter space is the wrong space in which to measure gradient steps — what matters is how much the model's output distribution changes, which is measured by the Fisher information matrix. Adam's per-parameter learning rate rescaling (dividing by √v_t) is a diagonal FIM approximation, which is why it fails on highly correlated parameters where off-diagonal FIM terms matter. The connection to TRPO/PPO is the most concrete production use case: constraining updates by KL[π_old ‖ π_new] rather than by Euclidean weight change is what makes RL training stable.`,
